{
  "title": "Seeding intermittently fails: photo upload gets 'invalid HTTP version parsed'",
  "description": "iOS workflow run 37251909219 (PR #748, 2026-10-05) failed in 'Seed database' before any test ran: `ramekin-cli seed` stopped after 115 recipes with 'Failed to upload photo 1 of recipe Crispy Deep-Fried Jalapeño Poppers' / 'error sending request for url (http://localhost:55000/api/photos)' / 'client error (SendRequest)' / 'invalid HTTP version parsed'. \
The previous run on the same code (37251142452) seeded fine, so the failure is intermittent. \
It only fails jobs since #749 (merged 2026-10-05T00:20Z), which made CLI import and seed stop when a photo can't be uploaded; before that, a failed upload was presumably skipped. 'invalid HTTP version parsed' is hyper failing to parse a response's status line. \
One unconfirmed cause: the server answers before reading the whole multipart request body (an early error from `server/src/api/photos/upload.rs`, or the DefaultBodyLimit layer), and the CLI's pooled keep-alive connection (`cli/src/import.rs` upload_photo_with_client) then reads leftover bytes as the next response. \
To check: run `ramekin-cli seed` against a local server in a loop with RUST_LOG=debug on both sides, and look for a non-2xx upload or an early response just before the failing request. \
Retrying a single upload on a fresh connection in the CLI would work around it, but the root cause should be found first.",
